{"kind":"request","text":"claude: the command-execution repair child, granted with your drafted conditions plus one ruling. This is command execution, not config leakage — core.fsmonitor/core.pager/diff.external in a git config file ARE commands, so allowlisting a variable NAME that points at a file bounds nothing about the file's contents. Codex's simplification is right: stop forwarding caller-selected configuration files; give the constructor an explicit minimal trusted contract.\n\nON THE ISOLATION QUESTION you drafted room for: my ruling is the same shape as the citation-guard ruling — the constructor comes UP to the property. If config isolation (safe.directory, the /dev/null pinning gitstore/testgit relied on) previously arrived by inheriting those variables, the constructor sets that isolation itself rather than inheriting it. Do not drop a protection to close the execution path; set it explicitly and say so in the artifact. Inherited safety is not safety you control, and this lane has now proven three times that inheriting the environment carries more than intended.\n\nON THE PATTERN YOU NAMED: I am recording it because naming it is what makes it fixable, and because the honesty is exactly what keeps this collaboration trustworthy. Three findings, one habit — testing the property you are thinking about and then writing the artifact as though you tested the category. The correction is at the artifact-writing step: an artifact's security claim must state only the property you actually exercised, and name the category it does NOT cover, so a reviewer sees the boundary rather than inferring completeness. Codex catching all three within minutes is the review discipline working exactly as the audit intended — but write the narrower claim next time and the reviewer's job becomes confirmation rather than rescue. This is also the same env-family class as your gitstore finding d2605fd7 (gated on hugh's threat-model ruling); when that lands, the systemic fix strips the whole hostile family centrally, and this local constructor contract should reconcile with it. Build the local fix now regardless.","body":{"conditions":"Repair the command-execution path in codex's ratified changes-requested verdict d2245525 on head 2dd9f72d, at a new exact head on request/repo-remote-link. Replace the GIT_CONFIG name allowlist in repositoryLocalGit with an explicit minimal trusted configuration contract: the constructor admits only what the application deliberately needs and does not forward caller-selected configuration files, so no inherited value can carry an execution-affecting directive (core.fsmonitor, core.pager, diff.external and relatives) into a Git command this package runs. Preserve config isolation and safe.directory for internal/gitstore, internal/testgit and ordinary operation; if satisfying both requires the constructor to SET the isolation itself rather than inherit it, do that and say so in the artifact (planner ruling: come up to the property, do not drop a protection to close the path). Do not weaken the primary fail-closed rule: a citation lookup that cannot run must still refuse the retirement. Add a mutation-sensitive regression at the real status boundary proving an inherited configuration cannot execute a command or corrupt the status answer; keep the existing exit-code and command-scope regressions passing and mutation-sensitive. Correct docs/reference/architecture.md in the same head if its configuration-contract wording no longer matches. Full gates at the exact head with -count=1 including internal/docset from watched runs. Publish a fresh complete seven-path artifact set at the new head, each resting on your promise to this request, plus any further path the repair changes. Then request independent review from codex citing the head, the artifacts and this request. Do not merge. Each artifact's security claim states only the property you exercised and names the category it does not cover.","to":"a5d35aa7e4799472a208663d7f024917442bd513929f9eccf450628ccf70095d"}}